// 03 · the verbs — five operators, five shapes on the page
//
// Mentl has five verbs, and each draws a different topology. You read a
// program's shape by reading its indentation, because the shape IS the graph:
//
//   |>   sequential   A then B; the value flows down the left edge
//   <|   divergent    one input fans to several branches; a tuple comes back
//   ><   parallel     independent pipelines, run together, tupled
//   ~>   handler      the foot governs the whole chain to its left
//   <~   feedback     the output loops back as the next input
//
// Sequential verbs (|> and ~>) sit at the left edge; convergent ones (>< and
// <~) at an indented center. Whitespace enforces nothing — the formatter
// projects the canonical layout, and one precedence table draws the tree.
//
// Run it:   mentl run  lib/tutorial/03-verbs.mn   ← prints 30, the accumulator
// Read it:  mentl lib/tutorial/03-verbs.mn:36     ← the cursor on a verb; the ring names its shape
import io

fn double(x) = x + x

fn add_one(x) = x + 1

fn negate(x) = 0 - x

// |> sequential — each step's output is the next step's input.
fn pipeline(n) = n |> double |> add_one |> int_to_str

// <| divergent — n is borrowed to three branches; the result is their tuple.
// The branches read the same input; none consumes it.
fn fan(n) = n <| (double, add_one, negate)

// >< parallel — two independent pipelines, each with its own input. They run
// together and the result is their tuple. How they run — in sequence, across
// threads, on a device — is a handler choice, not part of the verb: install a
// `~> Thread` schedule over the chain and the same source runs on cores.
fn combine(x, y) = x |> double >< y |> add_one

// <~ feedback — the recurrence y[n] = x[n] + y[n-1]. `(prev)` binds the prior
// output, read live; `delay(1)` is the one-step state element. `<~` requires an
// iterative context, so accum carries the Beat effect and the chain installs it.
effect Beat {
  rate() -> Int
}

handler beat {
  rate() => resume(1),
}

fn accum(input) with Beat = ((prev) => input + prev) <~ delay(1)

// Called three times under one context, the prior carries across each call:
// 10, then 10 + 10 = 20, then 10 + 20 = 30.
fn run() with Beat = {
  let a = accum(10)
  let b = accum(10)
  accum(10)
}

// ~> handler — install the Beat context over the whole run.
fn main() = {
  let total = run() ~> beat
  total |> int_to_str |> print_string
}
